[size=5][b]DLSS5oneclick[/b][/size]
[b]One click. The leaked DLSS 5 neural-rendering build in any DirectX 11/12 game, with or without DLSS of its own.[/b]
Single native Windows exe (Rust), no runtime, nothing to configure. Open source: [url=https://github.com/faisalkindi/DLSS5oneclick]github.com/faisalkindi/DLSS5oneclick[/url]

[size=4][b]What it does[/b][/size]
Pick the game folder, press [b]Install DLSS 5[/b]. The tool detects the game exe (Unity / Unreal helpers skipped), works out whether the game already ships DLSS and whether it renders with D3D11 or D3D12, then installs exactly what that case needs — every file downloaded from its upstream at install time; the only third-party content inside the exe is three SIL-OFL fonts.

[list]
[*][b]Game already has DLSS[/b] → ReShade (add-on build, as dxgi.dll) + the DLSS 5 add-on (renodx-dlss5.addon64 + nvngx_dlssnr.dll). The add-on hooks the game's own DLSS calls. [b]DX11 games[/b] also get NIGos' dlss5-bridge.
[*][b]Game has no DLSS[/b] → ReShade + shader headers + DLSS5-Feeder + LumeniteFX motion vectors + the DLSS 5 add-on, and ReShade.ini / ReShadePreset.ini written so LumeniteFX Kernel feeds DLSS5_Feed with DLSS5_MV_PROVIDER=3. This is the DSOGaming guide, automated.
[/list]
Re-running only fetches what is missing. Since 0.5.1 downloads no longer go through the GitHub API, so the 60-per-hour cap that caused [i]HTTP 403 Forbidden[/i] after a few installs is gone; github.com itself must be reachable (some countries block it — a proxy/VPN is the only way).

[b]New in 0.5.0 — engine choice for games with native DLSS:[/b] besides the default ReShade + RenoDX add-on, you can pick [url=https://github.com/Dagherbou/OptiScaler_DLSSNR]Dagherbou's OptiScaler_DLSSNR[/url] (OptiScaler with a built-in Neural Rendering pass). The tool extracts it as dxgi.dll, adds the DLSS 5 model, and Remove takes it out via a manifest. In game: [b]Insert[/b] opens the OptiScaler overlay, enable Neural Rendering there. The two engines cannot share one game.

[b]New in 0.8.0 — RenoDX HDR mods and RE Engine games:[/b] when the tool recognises the game (Steam app id, else folder/exe name, matched against the RenoDX games index and wiki) a [b]RenoDX HDR mod[/b] checkbox appears with the mod's status and the wiki's note; tick it and the game's renodx-<game>.addon64 is installed beside the DLSS 5 add-on (they coexist: different add-on names, different ReShade.ini sections — verified in Expedition 33 and Dragon's Dogma 2). One RenoDX game mod per game; the tool refuses if another is already there and Remove only takes the one it placed. Works with either engine: on OptiScaler the ReShade DLL goes in as ReShade64.dll with LoadReshade=true in OptiScaler.ini (OptiScaler's documented way to run ReShade add-ons beside it; untested in-game by the author). Turn Windows AutoHDR / RTX HDR off with these mods. RE Engine games (Resident Evil, DMC5, Monster Hunter, SF6, Dragon's Dogma 2, Pragmata) now get praydog's REFramework dinput8.dll first, without which ReShade crashes them. Unreal games with a bootstrapper exe in the root (Expedition 33) now resolve to the -Shipping exe and find the DLSS the game keeps under Plugins.

[b]Remove[/b] takes out what the tool added (ReShade stays); [b]Remove incl. ReShade[/b] also deletes dxgi.dll, the ReShade ini files and reshade-shaders — refused if it finds add-ons or shaders the tool did not install. The game's own nvngx_dlss.dll is never touched.

[size=4][b]Use[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Run dlss5oneclick.exe
[*][b]Games[/b] page: every installed Steam / Epic / GOG / Xbox game, newest first, with its poster, DirectX chip and DLSS status — click one. Or [b]Add a folder[/b] → pick any install folder
[*][b]Install DLSS 5[/b]
[*]In game: [b]Home[/b] opens ReShade → [b]DLSS 5 Neural Rendering[/b] panel → enable. [b]F6[/b] toggles neural rendering, [b]F5[/b] takes its screenshot. Keep the game's MSAA/SSAA off.
[/list]
Command line: [font=Courier New]dlss5oneclick.exe "C:\Games\Foo"[/font] · [font=Courier New]--check[/font] shows what it detected without installing · [font=Courier New]--remove[/font] uninstalls · [font=Courier New]--remove-all[/font] also removes ReShade · [font=Courier New]--engine=opti[/font] picks the OptiScaler engine · [font=Courier New]--renodx[/font] also installs the game's RenoDX HDR mod.

[size=4][b]Not handled[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]32-bit games (the Feeder's host64 setup is not automated — the tool refuses rather than half-install)
[*]DirectX 9 and Vulkan games — the Feeder and NIGos' bridge do support them by hand, this tool does not automate those setups yet
[*]Non-NVIDIA and GTX/GT cards — refused up front: the model needs tensor cores and NGX. RTX 50 runs it at full speed, RTX 40 with a patched build at moderate cost, RTX 20/30 through an FP16 path at heavy cost (the status line shows your tier)
[*]Online games — refused when Easy Anti-Cheat, BattlEye or GameGuard files are found in the install (Helldivers 2 etc.): ReShade add-on injection is what they flag, kick at best, ban at worst. Games that let you switch the anti-cheat off for offline play (GTA V: BattlEye unticked in the Rockstar Games Launcher) get a checkbox under the warning to install anyway, at your own risk
[*]API detection can come back unknown on monolithic Unreal exes; DX12 is then assumed (shown in the status line) — a DX11 game in that state would miss the bridge
[/list]

[size=4][b]Windows Defender[/b][/size]
The exe is not code-signed and it downloads DLLs into game folders, so Defender/SmartScreen may complain. If it quarantines the tool or the add-on files it placed in a game, restore them from Protection history, exclude the game folder, and press Install again (it only re-fetches what is missing). Source is public on GitHub; every release is built from it.

[size=4][b]Known issues[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Feeder path + exclusive fullscreen: every focus change recreates the swapchain and DLSS5-Feeder can crash on the rebuild (Feeder issue #16, upstream). Use borderless/windowed. Raising create_delay in dlss5-feed.cfg helps.
[*]Neural rendering at native 4K costs several ms; with v-sync on at 60 Hz that shows as a drop to 30 fps. Turn v-sync off or lower work_resolution in dlss5-feed.cfg.
[*]The DLSS 5 add-on and its model are a leaked, closed-source build hosted by a third party; this tool downloads them as-is and cannot vouch for them.
[/list]

